  "body": "When a language model predicts the next word, it assigns a raw score to every possible choice. A function turns these scores into percentages that add up to one hundred. Top-k filtering removes all choices except the top few preferred ones.\n\nLook at the bar heights in the chart to see how removing low choices changes the final values. The options that are dropped fall to zero, and the remaining options share the remaining space.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nBecause the total must add up to one hundred percent, dividing the space among fewer options makes each surviving option grow larger. The reported probability of any chosen token increases after filtering. This means the final logged number is higher than the model originally computed before filtering started.",
