  "body": "When a language model generates text, it often applies top-k filtering to restrict sampling to the top $k$ most likely candidates. The raw probabilities from the initial softmax output are adjusted because the discarded tokens are removed entirely. Look at the chart to see how the relative proportions shift when the remaining values are rescaled.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nTo ensure the new probabilities sum to $1$, the model renormalizes the kept values by dividing each by the sum of the retained probabilities. This means the reported probability of the chosen token increases compared to its original softmax score. The underlying mechanism scales up every surviving candidate by the same constant factor.\n\nUnderstanding this adjustment is crucial when logging generation metrics or computing exact token likelihoods in constrained sampling routines. The final reported value no longer reflects the true global probability from the full distribution. Instead, it represents a conditional probability within the restricted subset.",
