],
"body": "A confusion matrix tabulates how many instances fall into each true‑vs‑predicted category. The rows show the actual class, the columns the predicted class.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nIn this example the top‑left cell (36) is true negatives, top‑right (8) false positives, bottom‑left (45) false negatives, and bottom‑right (60) true positives. From these numbers we compute precision = $\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}=\\frac{60}{68}=0.88$ and recall = $\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}=\\frac{60}{105}=0.57$.\n\nThese rates summarise classifier performance: high precision means few false alarms, while high recall means few missed detections. Both are needed for balanced evaluation.",
"questions": [
"What does a high false‑positive count imply for a model?",
"How can we adjust a classifier to improve recall?",
"Why might precision and recall trade off against each other?"
],
"terms": [
{
"t": "precision",
"d": "The proportion of predicted positives that are actually positive: TP/(TP+FP).",
"n": 2
},
{
"t": "recall",
"d": "The proportion of actual positives that are correctly predicted: TP/(TP+FN).",
"n": 2
}
]
}

